  "slug": "microglial-dependent-2f65",
  "summary": "Isogenic stem cell models and arrayed Perturb-seq now map genetic control of microglial state and function.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is control of myeloid immune cells in development, state and injury. Two microglia-focused studies use human stem cell models: an isogenic NGLY1-deficient line in which NGLY1 loss raises RUNX1 and alters microglial development, phagocytosis and pro-inflammatory cytokine expression, and an arrayed Perturb-seq platform in TF-iMG microglia for mapping state regulation and target identification. Neutrophil papers extend the theme: G alpha 15 is identified as a degranulation-specific effector of FPR1 and LTB4R, and neutrophils are required for Müller glia reprogramming after stab injury in zebrafish retina but not after NMDA injury. A fifth preprint links kidney-derived osteopontin, cleaved by lung thrombin, to hippocampal CCL2 upregulation, monocyte recruitment and delirium-like dysfunction after acute kidney injury. Collectively, these preprints examine myeloid cell regulation across perturbation and injury contexts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
